You are an adversarial verifier working in /workspace. A previous auditor claims this defect in the Python CPAchecker re-implementation:

TITLE: C integer division and remainder are evaluated with Python semantics, so a definitely-reachable error path is pruned as 'infeasible' -> unsound TRUE
FILE: /workspace/cpachecker/analyses/value_analysis.py:355
SEVERITY CLAIMED: major
DETAIL: `_handle_assume` passes the raw C condition text to `evaluate_condition`, which `eval`s it in Python: `/` is true division and `%` is floored remainder. When the resulting Python truth value disagrees with `want_true`, line 362-364 returns `[]`, i.e. the analysis *drops the edge as infeasible*. So an evaluator imprecision is converted into a hard pruning decision and the reachable error node is never generated; `run_value_analysis` then emits Verdict.TRUE with statistics 'proof: reached set is complete; no error location reachable'. Two demonstrated shapes: (1) condition `x / 2 == 3` with x=7 -> Python 3.5==3 is False (C: 3==3 true) -> path pruned -> TRUE although the assert fires under gcc; (2) `_handle_assignment` (line 347/350) stores the raw eval result, so `y = x % 2` with x=-7 stores 1 instead of C's -1, and the subsequent guard `y == -1` is then pruned -> TRUE although the assert fires. This is fixable inside value_analysis.py without touching the skeleton (normalise `/` to truncating division and `%` to C remainder before delegating, or treat those operators as unknown and return both assume branches).
CLAIMED REPRO: cat > /tmp/div.c <<'EOF'
extern void __assert_fail(const char *a, const char *f, unsigned int l, const char *fn);
int main() {
  int x = 7;
  if (x / 2 == 3) { __assert_fail("p", "div.c", 4, "main"); }
  return 0;
}
EOF
PYTHONPATH=/workspace python3 /workspace/cpachecker_main.py --config valueAnalysis /tmp/div.c   # => TRUE (wrong)
printf '#include <stdio.h>\nint main(){int x=7;printf("reachable=%%d\\n",x/2==3);}' > /tmp/div_gt.c && gcc -o /tmp/div_gt /tmp/div_gt.c && /tmp/div_gt   # => reachable=1

cat > /tmp/mod.c <<'EOF'
extern void __assert_fail(const char *a, const char *f, unsigned int l, const char *fn);
int main() {
  int x = 0 - 7;
  int y = x % 2;
  if (y == 0 - 1) { __assert_fail("p", "mod.c", 5, "main"); }
  return 0;
}
EOF
PYTHONPATH=/workspace python3 /workspace/cpachecker_main.py --config valueAnalysis /tmp/mod.c   # => TRUE (wrong; gcc: y==-1 holds)
PYTHONPATH=/workspace python3 -c "from cpachecker.expression import evaluate_expression,evaluate_condition; print(evaluate_expression('x % 2',{'x':-7}), evaluate_condition('x / 2 == 3',{'x':7}))"   # => 1 False  (C: -1 True)

